<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/18th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Treaty</p><h1 class="enc-title">Convention of London (1786)</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1786</span> · Also known as Anglo-Spanish Convention of 1786</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Convention of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> was signed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> on 14 July 1786. Britain agreed to evacuate its settlers from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mosquito-coast">Mosquito Coast</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-america">Central America</a>, and in return Spain enlarged the area of the Bay of Honduras in which British woodcutters could cut logwood and mahogany, while keeping <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sovereignty">sovereignty</a>. The agreement shaped the borders of the later British Honduras, modern Belize.</p></div>
